34:44.729 --> 34:48.606
definitions regarding general landscape as it relates to wildland

34:48.720 --> 34:52.011
urban interface. This will be presented by Beth Fuchs.

34:54.049 --> 34:57.673
Thank you and good evening, Commissioners. And just a quick explanation for

34:58.173 --> 35:01.530
those in the audience who may not be familiar with the WUI, those are areas

35:01.723 --> 35:05.580
where development is adjacent to wildlands that are undeveloped,

35:06.046 --> 35:09.276
and they have a higher wildfire risk because of

35:09.776 --> 35:14.355
their location. So that— talking about areas that

35:14.420 --> 35:17.940
are— have certain standards because of that heightened fire risk.

35:18.583 --> 35:21.607
And tonight, this item has kind of 2 parts to it.

35:22.363 --> 35:25.775
One of it is by the direction of the They passed some

35:26.275 --> 35:29.950
legislation recently that we need to adopt, and the other part is at the direction

35:30.450 --> 35:34.606
of the city council. And I had previously bought brought an item forward

35:35.106 --> 35:38.909
to the commission for discussion that had proposed some setback standards

35:39.487 --> 35:43.373
at the recommendation of fire officials. But when I took that to the city council

35:43.873 --> 35:47.885
and they were reviewing it, they had some concerns, especially with being

35:47.933 --> 35:51.754
able to fully utilize their property. So they gave direction,

35:52.251 --> 35:56.039
different direction. And so that's what we're bringing forward tonight.

35:56.520 --> 35:59.618
So that first part, those, the state changes,

36:00.292 --> 36:03.887
they just want it to be so that if someone lives next

36:04.387 --> 36:08.284
to wildlands and wants to remove landscaping in order to provide better

36:08.300 --> 36:12.216
fire protection for their home, that we don't have any standards

36:12.328 --> 36:15.859
that would prohibit them from doing so. So just need to put

36:16.359 --> 36:19.841
that in. And then the city council, they decided instead

36:19.953 --> 36:23.453
of putting blanket standards for everywhere,

36:23.566 --> 36:26.665
they wanted to be able to evaluate it on a case-by-case basis.

36:27.339 --> 36:30.791
And so in order to do that, we need to make sure that they're aware

36:31.291 --> 36:34.886
when something is in the WUI. And they also want to try to have residents

36:35.386 --> 36:37.840
know that they're going to be in the WUI, especially because the state will be

36:38.340 --> 36:42.175
coming out and assessing those properties to help them better protect.

36:42.240 --> 36:45.806
Protect them and have no strategies and things they can do to better

36:46.306 --> 36:49.213
protect their homes. And so some of the ways we're going to do that is

36:49.325 --> 36:52.764
one, we're going to add notations to final plats. And then

36:52.940 --> 36:56.491
also we on a staff level have changed

36:56.991 --> 37:00.589
in our processes where every development application that comes through, we're going to

37:01.089 --> 37:03.995
check and see if it's in a WUI location. And if it is, you and

37:04.027 --> 37:07.498
the city council will be informed of that when you're reviewing the application so

37:07.998 --> 37:10.276
you know whether or not to me. That should be a consideration.

37:11.336 --> 37:15.478
And then I also added some definitions just to support those changes.

37:16.329 --> 37:20.103
And so the first one's pretty straightforward, just on final plats.

37:20.424 --> 37:23.362
When they go forward, they just need to note that it's in the WUI.

37:24.117 --> 37:27.938
