01:24:32.360 --> 01:24:35.110
OK. Oh, I got a question. Yeah, for sure.

01:24:35.830 --> 01:24:39.750
Just more out of just curiosity how this works. But there's a provision

01:24:40.250 --> 01:24:42.890
in there for road maintenance reimbursements during the project. Yeah.

01:24:43.740 --> 01:24:47.477
learned through experience with these data centers is that

01:24:47.702 --> 01:24:50.477
the construction traffic can do damage to our roads.

01:24:51.135 --> 01:24:55.097
And so that's what that's about, is them reimbursing us for the costs

01:24:55.482 --> 01:24:58.867
that the city incurs through that damage to the roads during the construction process.

01:24:58.932 --> 01:25:02.028
I guess my question was, how do we determine what that is? And,

01:25:02.525 --> 01:25:05.862
um, you know, that's something that, uh, when we determine,

01:25:06.375 --> 01:25:08.878
you know, so when you, when you build a road, right,

01:25:09.199 --> 01:25:12.153
there's sort of a standard for the useful life of that road. Right.

01:25:12.233 --> 01:25:15.879
And so when that thing— when the road has to be repaired early, then we're

01:25:16.379 --> 01:25:19.783
like, we can look at, okay, what construction traffic's going through there, is it,

01:25:20.184 --> 01:25:24.119
you know, who's it coming from, and being able to say,

01:25:24.200 --> 01:25:27.252
all right, well, we need to repair this road, you know,

01:25:27.428 --> 01:25:31.059
3 years early. That's gonna exact,

01:25:31.107 --> 01:25:33.629
you know, you know, it's a 15-year road or whatever. I don't know what the

01:25:34.129 --> 01:25:37.675
standards are. I'm just throwing out hypotheticals. But so

01:25:37.948 --> 01:25:41.158
that's 20% early. And so now we have to, you know,

01:25:41.174 --> 01:25:44.127
so that's essentially a 20% increase in cost to the city.

01:25:44.207 --> 01:25:47.466
Sure. And seeking reimbursement based on allocation of

01:25:47.738 --> 01:25:51.478
who's responsible for what. You know, what— because we know,

01:25:51.735 --> 01:25:55.250
like, we know how much, like, you know, for example, you know, QTS,

01:25:55.266 --> 01:25:58.813
how much construction traffic is related to them, or Metta for their

01:25:59.313 --> 01:26:01.525
ongoing construction, Williams. Right.

01:26:02.034 --> 01:26:05.565
So, you know, we look at that, we see these trucks going down, we know

01:26:05.936 --> 01:26:09.580
what's going down there. Our business— or our building inspectors are down there daily.

01:26:11.004 --> 01:26:15.583
You know, I feel like they live there during the business hours. And so,

01:26:17.270 --> 01:26:20.615
you know, so we're able to— we haven't had any issues

01:26:20.712 --> 01:26:25.584
with that, to be honest. We've had good partners with— partnerships

01:26:26.084 --> 01:26:29.231
with the data center companies. And And making sure that we're made whole based on

01:26:29.731 --> 01:26:33.840
that. Perfect. Let's see.

01:26:34.340 --> 01:26:37.032
So a couple of questions, like on 3.2, new taxes.

01:26:37.854 --> 01:26:41.255
I mean, the agreement— so there's a— the development

01:26:41.755 --> 01:26:45.444
agreement would define in this 3.2, the city shall

01:26:45.944 --> 01:26:49.470
not, during the term of the agreement, recommend or support any

01:26:49.970 --> 01:26:53.565
new taxes. Yeah, I mean, we're in government. Specifically to the project.

01:26:55.069 --> 01:26:58.710
Yeah, so— Okay. Yeah, so this language is applicable

01:26:59.210 --> 01:27:01.823
solely and exclusively to the project, the property, or the data center industry, right?

01:27:02.304 --> 01:27:05.833
So we're already bound by that language in other data center agreements.

01:27:06.170 --> 01:27:09.282
So the only thing that would be different here is specific to the project,

01:27:09.427 --> 01:27:12.892
and we're not targeting any one project with taxes anyways.

01:27:13.373 --> 01:27:16.918
I don't see that as an actual give on the city's part. Saying that we're

01:27:17.418 --> 01:27:20.752
not going to advocate for taxing the data center industry, again, we're already constrained Contractually

01:27:20.833 --> 01:27:24.074
bound by that through other agreements anyways that we made in years past.

01:27:25.566 --> 01:27:29.127
But I mean, again, we're partnering with these companies

01:27:29.627 --> 01:27:33.106
to come in, and so it would be obviously not a great show

01:27:33.170 --> 01:27:37.037
of good faith to then say, all right, now hike up their taxes, you know,

01:27:37.262 --> 01:27:40.952
specifically targeted at them. Again, general tax increases that

01:27:41.452 --> 01:27:44.048
are going to affect them are still going to— those are things we feel free

01:27:44.096 --> 01:27:47.394
if we're going to go through, you know, in taxation and raise property

01:27:47.894 --> 01:27:51.558
taxes hypothetically, then that obviously is

01:27:51.574 --> 01:27:54.706
going to affect the data center just like everyone else. Okay. Thank you for that

01:27:54.738 --> 01:27:58.292
clarification. I had a couple questions. Like, if you

01:27:58.792 --> 01:28:01.214
think about like the impact fees under 3.3,

01:28:02.254 --> 01:28:05.156
if the project is phased,

01:28:06.302 --> 01:28:09.546
and right now there's— based on what I

01:28:10.046 --> 01:28:13.344
see, there's not specific phases, but Are the impact

01:28:13.844 --> 01:28:17.527
fees for this development agreement based on the

01:28:17.591 --> 01:28:21.004
impact fees at the time of the agreement?

01:28:22.062 --> 01:28:26.164
So that's not the way impact fees work. Impact fees are imposed at

01:28:26.664 --> 01:28:30.812
the time of the building permit application. Okay. Okay. So that's

01:28:30.828 --> 01:28:33.937
the controlling date. And we— so whenever we

01:28:34.437 --> 01:28:38.073
go through the process to alter or modify our impact fees, For any

01:28:38.089 --> 01:28:40.642
particular type of impact fee, say, you know, again,

01:28:40.754 --> 01:28:44.496
transportation impact fees. We're gonna go through the process. It's a very

01:28:45.009 --> 01:28:48.574
long process that the state mandates.

01:28:49.425 --> 01:28:52.829
And then even after we've gone through all that, there's a 90-day period

01:28:52.861 --> 01:28:56.490
between when we impose a new impact fee and when it actually takes effect.

01:28:57.132 --> 01:29:00.456
And so that is sort of like the equivalent of like a vesting that you

01:29:00.488 --> 01:29:03.619
would see in other land use aspects. Is that all right?

